<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corpus Christi (feast)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1246</span> · Also known as Feast of Corpus Christi, Corpus Christi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corpus Christi is a Christian feast honouring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>, celebrated on the Thursday after Trinity Sunday. It began at Liège in 1246 after the visions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juliana-of-cornillon">Juliana of Cornillon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> extended it to the whole Church in 1264. It is marked by processions with the consecrated host, and Orvieto Cathedral is linked to it through the miracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsena">Bolsena</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast.jpg" alt="Corpus Christi (feast)" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Emil Doepler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_Emil_Doepler_Fronleichnamsprozession.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
